## Abstract

Synesthesia is defined by the field that studies it as an inducer that automatically and
involuntarily evokes a concurrent experience not present in the stimulus, consistently over time.
It is treated as a rare, largely congenital condition (about 4% of people for any type), certified
by consistency tests and by interference in Stroop-type tasks. Every literate human meets every one
of these criteria for written language. A literate person cannot not read a word (Stroop, 1935);
seeing a word evokes its sound, the inner voice, measurable in auditory voice areas during silent
reading; the coupling is consistent by construction and runs in both directions, so that spoken
words activate their spelling; and it reorganizes perception of the inducer's own domain, since
phonemic awareness of speech arises only with alphabetic literacy. The coupling is not inherited.
It is built, reliably, by a procedure (schooling), in children and in adults, in most of humanity.
Literacy is therefore a synesthesia between visual patterns and sounds that already carried
meaning, and its existence at scale is a constructive proof that synesthesia is trained: the
capacity is exhibited by its construction, with billions of witnesses. Language, in this light, is
a cognitive technology for constructing senses evolution did not supply, which is what the author
has called synthetic synesthesia. The evidence for every premise was published by the fields
themselves; the inference was not drawn.

