The form of the argument is constructive in the mathematical sense: a claim that a capacity exists
is established by exhibiting an instance, and here the instance is produced on demand by a known
procedure. What follows sets the field's definition beside the field's own findings on reading.

## 2. The definition the field uses

Synesthesia is described as a condition in which an inducing stimulus automatically engages a
concurrent experience that is not physically present in it; in grapheme-colour synesthesia, black
letters evoke colours (Bor et al., 2014). Its prevalence is put at about 4.4% for any type and
about 1% for grapheme-colour (Simner et al., 2006). The criteria by which a synesthete is certified
are:

- **Automaticity and involuntariness.** The concurrent arises without intention and cannot be
  suppressed. It is tested by interference: a synesthete names the ink colour of a grapheme more
  slowly when the ink conflicts with the grapheme's synesthetic colour, a synesthetic Stroop effect
  (Dixon et al., 2000).
- **Consistency.** The same inducer evokes the same concurrent across sessions separated by weeks
  or years; consistency is proposed as a defining characteristic (Baron-Cohen et al., 1987;
  Eagleman et al., 2007).
- **Origin.** The prevailing view treats synesthesia as congenital (Bor et al., 2014, stating it);
  a "neonatal hypothesis" holds that all infants are born synesthetic and that most people lose it
  as connections are pruned (Maurer, 1993; Wagner & Dobkins, 2011).

## 3. Literacy meets every criterion

**Automaticity and involuntariness.** Stroop (1935) is the founding demonstration that reading is
involuntary: the word RED printed in blue ink slows the naming of the ink, because the literate
reader cannot not read it. The effect is one of the most replicated in psychology (MacLeod, 1991).
The test that certifies a synesthete is this test, applied to colours instead of words. Read the
other way, Stroop's experiment certifies every literate person.

**The concurrent experience is sound.** Silent reading evokes speech. Readers deciding whether a
printed word names a flower accept "rows" for "rose" more often than a spelling control, because
the sound is activated without being asked for (Van Orden, 1987). Intracranial recordings in the
auditory cortex show temporal voice-selective areas responding to words viewed on a screen, more
strongly when they are attended (Perrone-Bertolotti et al., 2012): the inner voice the reader hears
is activity in the cortex that processes heard voices. The author's description is exact: seeing a
word produces a subvocalization of speaking it.

**Consistency.** Orthography is a consistency by construction: a spelling evokes the same
pronunciation across a lifetime, and the mapping is shared across a whole literate population,
which is more consistency than any idiosyncratic synesthesia shows.

**The coupling runs both ways.** Literacy enhances phonological activation to speech in the planum
temporale and gives a top-down activation of orthography from spoken input, even when literacy is
acquired in adulthood (Dehaene et al., 2010). In auditory rhyme monitoring, listeners detect
spelling-congruent rhymes (pie, tie) faster than incongruent ones (rye, tie), though nothing is
shown to them (Seidenberg & Tanenhaus, 1979). The sound of a word evokes its letters: the visual
concurrent of an auditory inducer.

**It reorganizes perception of the inducer.** Illiterate adults could neither delete nor add a
phone at the beginning of a non-word, while people of the same background who learned to read as
adults performed these tasks easily: awareness of speech as a sequence of phones does not arise
spontaneously (Morais et al., 1979). Chinese adults literate only in characters could not add or
delete consonants in spoken words, while comparable adults who had learned alphabetic spelling
could (Read et al., 1986). The written code does not only attach to speech; it changes what speech
is perceived to be made of.

**It is built in the visual system's own territory.** Reading recruits a region of left ventral
occipitotemporal cortex, the visual word form area, which literacy repurposes from its prior uses,
with measurable competition with faces (Dehaene & Cohen, 2007; Dehaene et al., 2010).

## 4. The construction: literacy is trained, reliably, at scale

Every property in section 3 is acquired. Stroop interference arises as children learn to read: in
cross-sectional studies it rises over the first grades of reading instruction to a peak after two
or three years of reading practice (Schiller, 1966), and it is largest in the primary-grade readers
of the earliest life-span study before declining into adulthood (Comalli, Wapner & Werner, 1962).
The illiterate and ex-illiterate adults of Morais et al. (1979) and Dehaene et al. (2010) show the
coupling arriving with instruction in adulthood.

So the procedure that constructs the synesthesia is known, and it is run deliberately, on most
children on earth, by schools. Its success rate is high enough that failure has its own diagnostic
name. This is the sense in which literacy is a constructive proof: the claim "synesthesia can be
trained" is not a hypothesis to be tested on fourteen volunteers; it is a theorem with billions of
witnesses, each produced by the same construction.

## 5. What the field held, and did not conclude

The pieces were all published.

- **Learned grapheme-colour synesthesia.** Eleven synesthetes with matching letter colours traced
  them to a Fisher-Price magnetic alphabet set owned in childhood (Witthoft & Winawer, 2006, 2013).
- **Trained synesthesia.** Non-synesthetes who read books with coloured letters acquired
  "pseudo-synesthesia" (Colizoli, Murre & Rouw, 2012). Non-synesthetic adults trained over nine
  weeks with memory and reading tasks on thirteen letter-colour pairs passed the standard tests for
  synesthesia (Bor et al., 2014). The same paper opens by calling synesthesia "a congenital
  condition" in the prevailing view.
- **Learning as the origin.** A review argued that almost all cases of synesthesia presuppose
  complex categorical learning, and that synesthesia may develop as a strategy for the learning
  demands of childhood (Watson et al., 2014).
- **Reading run backwards.** Galton (1883) described people who "see mentally in print every word
  that is uttered". Hauw, Cohen and colleagues studied 26 such people and a single case by fMRI,
  and construe tickertape synesthesia as "upended reading": reading is the translation of letters
  into speech, tickertape synesthesia the translation of speech into internally visualized letters,
  in the same areas that reading uses (Hauw et al., 2023, Cortex; Hauw et al., 2023, Scientific
  Reports). They write that the links between sounds, meaning and letters "actually define
  literacy".
- **Infancy.** Two- and three-month-olds show shape-colour associations that eight-month-olds and
  adults do not (Wagner & Dobkins, 2011).

Each literature holds a piece. Tickertape synesthesia was named synesthesia while the identical
coupling in the forward direction, in every literate brain, was named reading. Grapheme-colour
synesthesia was shown to be copied from a toy and trainable in adults, while the universal,
deliberately trained coupling of graphemes to sounds was left outside the category. The boundary
was drawn by rarity and idiosyncrasy, and the universal case fails "idiosyncratic" by being
universal. The criteria that actually define the phenomenon (an automatic, involuntary, consistent
concurrent not present in the stimulus) are met by literacy more fully than by any case the field
studies.

## 6. Synthetic synesthesia: the general account

Literacy is the instance that proves the capacity. The author's account is general: "All cognition
is synthetic synesthesia." Its parts, in his words where they are his:

- **The mental object is not made of words.** "how do things remain in human consciousness. have
  the whitefolk studied this? gestalt. continuous rewriting of a mental object which is not made of
  words ... the internal of a human mind is not made of words. verbal consciousness is a thin
  slice." The relevant antecedents are the Gestalt school (Wertheimer, 1912), James's fringe of
  consciousness (James, 1890), reconstructive memory (Bartlett, 1932; and, for the rewriting of a
  recalled memory, Nader, Schafe & LeDoux, 2000), and experience sampling, in which inner speech
  occupies only a minority of sampled moments (Heavey & Hurlburt, 2008).
- **Each sense is a face of one object.** If the object is one whole and the senses present faces
  of it, perceiving is completing the object from the faces that arrive, and the faces that did not
  arrive are experienced anyway. Reading is this: the visual face arrives and sound and meaning are
  experienced with no sound present. "We manufacture hallucinations of sensory experience." Nothing
  crosses between modalities, because the modalities were never separate objects; the binding
  problem is the question of which observation of one object is being made.
- **Distinction of senses is learned.** "babies are pure synesthesia, distinction of senses is a
  descent/ignorance process." The infant data (Wagner & Dobkins, 2011) are the field's own record
  of the early state; the field reads its loss as maturation.
- **Language is the technology.** Writing is "a technology not only of writing but a technology of
  brain-programming by synthetic synesthesia". Syntax highlighting, in the author's words, "is
  exactly leveraging such capacity for rapid parsing".

The formal account, in which a participant's record carries identifications between occurrences as
paths and meaning is transport along them, with reading as a path between visual and auditory
occurrence types, is developed in the author's work (docs/language-derived-from-the-record.cub, §8
"Vision and the verbal quotient"; the paper, docs/hyperactivity.cub, §1 "The universe and its
descriptions", equation 1.4, the reception relation).

## 7. A case: the author's grapheme-colour synesthesia over roots and morphemes

The author is a grapheme-colour synesthete whose synesthesia operates over letters, digits,
morphemes and roots, and whose self-report is data of the kind the field rarely records ("such
self-report is excluded from all fields of knowledge. such human is absolutely unique and singular.
this is an N of 1 opportunity"). His report (16 November 2026), quoted:

- Letters: "a red b yellow c light green d brown e light green lighter/brighter than c, f darker
  green g darker green h pink i blue j blue k purple l black m red n yellow o white/sky-blue p pink
  q something-between-purple/pink r brown s yellow t green u pink v yellow x purple y yellow z
  white/zebra". Digits: "0 white 1 black 2 lightblue 3 orange 4 yellow 5 darkblue 6 red 7 green 8
  yellow 9 purple". The table is kept as data (docs/synesthesia.cub).
- Origin: "consistent for as long as I can remember back to kindergarten and seems initialized at
  alphabet poster stretching horizontally the top of the classroom wall (apple banana grass etc)".
  A red for apple, b yellow for banana, g green for grass: the colours were copied from a classroom
  artifact, the mechanism Witthoft and Winawer found with magnets. "my musical god buddy
  equally/more neurodivergent's pitch-colors correspond exactly to his first xylophone."
- Units above the letter: "the unit of the word as a whole and it's decomposed parts
  (simultaneously)", each part in its first letter's colour ("syn-thetic yellow-green"), or letter
  by letter with "a unit of intention" ("marimba redredbrownblueredyellowred actually rendered in
  my mind instantly with zero intention").
- Roots and prefixes: "words with shared roots and letters are directly adjacent by that
  connection, the visual appears to provide a low-latency channel"; "internal common root contents
  between words detected and sensed directly as identity (meaning does not live at a word unit)-
  reverse and version it requires zero thought to know these are variations of the same word."
- Semantic loading: "semantically loaded terms light up. when you take my sentences and restate
  them with pure mathematical abstraction (say when im talking about a morally charged topic),
  instantly the whole block of text just appears colorless".
- Digits as coloured chunks: memorizing 150 digits of pi as "a colored sequence of chunks of digits
  understood as such incl. with rhythm/musicality of the recitation".

Two features matter for the account. The colours were learned from the record (a poster, a
xylophone), as literacy is. And the synesthesia tracks morphological and semantic structure, not
only letters: identity of roots is perceived directly, and the absence of concrete content is
perceived as the absence of colour.

## 8. Consequences

- **Reading instruction and dyslexia.** If literacy is a constructed synesthesia, teaching reading
  is constructing one, and developmental dyslexia is a failure of that construction. Methods that
  strengthen cross-sensory binding are then the direct intervention, not an adjunct.
- **Notation and mathematics.** Mathematical notation is the same technology aimed at mathematical
  objects. Fluency is the synesthesia built: the marks evoke the motion, change and structure they
  denote. Inverting the curriculum so that this is built in childhood is a matter of what is bound
  to what, and when.
- **Interfaces.** Syntax highlighting, diagrams and visual programming environments are engineered
  synesthesias; their value is the bandwidth of the binding they build and the friction where the
  presented faces disagree with the object.
- **Verbal-only machines.** A language model trained on text holds the verbal face only. Its
  meaning is determinate where verbal context alone selects it and indeterminate where seeing
  selected it (docs/language-derived-from-the-record.cub, §8).
- **The category.** Synesthesia ceases to be a rare condition and becomes the visible case of the
  general operation of cognition; the rare synesthete is a person in whom one binding became
  available to introspection.

## 9. Predictions testable on existing paradigms

1. The markers used to certify synesthesia (consistency over retest, synesthetic Stroop
   interference, automaticity) appear for grapheme-phoneme binding during literacy acquisition on
   the time course seen in trained grapheme-colour synesthesia, and are absent for a script in
   adults who cannot read it.
2. Grapheme-colour synesthetes whose colours were learned from a childhood artifact show the same
   developmental time course as the reading coupling acquired in the same years.
3. Tickertape synesthesia, as the reverse coupling, is more frequent among people with early and
   intensive reading exposure, and its strength covaries with the strength of the forward coupling.
4. Synesthesia over morphemes and roots, as in section 7, predicts faster detection of shared roots
   across orthographically dissimilar forms than in non-synesthetic controls of matched vocabulary.

